sink 9778000

How to read the columns.
  `CPU ns/RPC` is getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF) MINUS the server handler's own CPU, both
  measured per round and divided by the RPCs in it. It counts every thread in the
  process, so the core's tokio workers are in it and grpc++'s transport is too; a
  per-thread sum over the threads the harness spawned is NOT like-for-like and
  biases the ratio by about 0.2 (C11 in this slice's defect log).
  `hostthr` is what N in flight costs the arm in HOST threads. Blocking needs N of
  them by construction -- a thread inside `ak_call_unary` is inside the core until
  the response lands. The callback and the queue need one. That is the deliveries'
  real difference and it is why the wall-clock ratio at 8 and 16 is not a like-for-
  like column: one host thread cannot decode 16 responses at once.
  `wall/A` is printed beside the CPU column and is never the headline (R9).
  `spr%` is the arm's own round-to-round spread on the CPU column, (max-min)/min.
  It is 5 to 25 percent here, which is why every DIFFERENCE below the table is a
  within-round delta and not a difference of two best-of-nine figures.

What is inside the numbers and is not the thing being measured.
  Thread creation. The blocking arms, `cb x N`, cell A and cell D spawn their host
  threads per round, so a round of 80 RPCs at 16 in flight pays 16 pthread_create
  and join pairs. At a generous 30 us each that is 480 us over 80 RPCs, about 6 us
  per RPC against a call of roughly 4,000 us -- 0.15 percent. It is arithmetic and
  not a measurement, and it is stated because it lands on exactly the arms the
  delivery comparison is between. It is two orders of magnitude below the
  differences that separate and cannot account for any of them.
  Cell D's slice concatenation, printed per level above: grpc++ hands the generic
  path a slice list and the core's decoder needs one buffer, where protobuf parses
  straight off the list. Cell A does not pay it and cell D does.
  The server. It is in-process and its handler CPU is subtracted from every cell,
  but its transport threads are not and cannot be; they are the same work for all
  four cells, so they dilute every ratio toward 1 rather than favouring one.

What this grid does NOT measure, per R11.
  The ENCODE direction. The request is an empty message, so the codec difference
  here is a decode difference and nothing else. A grid with a large request would
  be a different measurement and this one does not stand in for it.
  Allocation per RPC, which design/SHAPES.md's RPC arm asks for beside CPU. Nothing
  here counts allocations, and a page-fault or RSS proxy dressed as an allocation
  count would be worse than the gap.
  Streaming, TLS, retry, deadlines, metadata, the status code as a number, a real
  network, failure injection, and the server side. Section 9's case is behavioural
  and this arm is not a test of it.

